10. VOID FUNCTIONS
------------------------------------------------------------

If a function does not return a value, use void.

Example:

void greet(void)
{
    printf("Hello\n");
}

Call:

greet();


IMPORTANT:

Prefer:

void greet(void)

instead of:

void greet()

In a function prototype, `void` inside the parentheses
explicitly means "no parameters".

In modern C, use:

int main(void)

rather than:

int main()


------------------------------------------------------------
11. RETURN STATEMENT
------------------------------------------------------------

The return statement:

    1. sends a value back
    2. terminates the current function

Example:

int square(int x)
{
    return x * x;
}

If:

square(5)

then:

return 25;


A void function can also use return without a value:

void check(int x)
{
    if (x < 0)
        return;

    printf("Positive or zero\n");
}


