13. PASS BY VALUE
------------------------------------------------------------

C uses pass-by-value for function arguments.

Example:

#include <stdio.h>

void change(int x)
{
    x = 100;
}

int main(void)
{
    int a = 10;

    change(a);

    printf("%d\n", a);

    return 0;
}

Output:

10

Why?

A copy of `a` is passed to change().

Memory concept:

main:

a = 10

       |
       | copy
       v

change():

x = 10

Changing x does not change a.


