16. ARRAYS AS FUNCTION ARGUMENTS
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

void display(int arr[], int n)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
    }
}

Call:

int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

display(arr, 5);


IMPORTANT:

When an array is passed to a function, it is adjusted to a
pointer to its first element.

Therefore:

void display(int arr[], int n)

is effectively similar to:

void display(int *arr, int n)


------------------------------------------------------------
17. ARRAY SIZE MUST GENERALLY BE PASSED SEPARATELY
------------------------------------------------------------

Incorrect assumption:

void display(int arr[])
{
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
}

This does NOT correctly determine the original array length
inside the function.

Why?

Inside the function parameter, `arr` is a pointer.

Correct:

void display(int arr[], int n)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
}


------------------------------------------------------------
18. MODIFYING AN ARRAY INSIDE A FUNCTION
------------------------------------------------------------

#include <stdio.h>

void doubleElements(int arr[], int n)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        arr[i] *= 2;
    }
}

int main(void)
{
    int arr[] = {1, 2, 3, 4};

    doubleElements(arr, 4);

    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);

    return 0;
}

Output:

2 4 6 8

The function modifies the original array elements.


