20. LOCAL VARIABLES
------------------------------------------------------------

A variable declared inside a function/block is local to that
scope.

Example:

void test(void)
{
    int x = 10;

    printf("%d", x);
}

`x` cannot be directly accessed from main().


------------------------------------------------------------
21. GLOBAL VARIABLES
------------------------------------------------------------

A variable declared outside all functions has file scope
(unless modified by other declarations/specifiers).

Example:

#include <stdio.h>

int count = 10;

void display(void)
{
    printf("%d\n", count);
}

int main(void)
{
    display();

    return 0;
}

Global variables have static storage duration.


------------------------------------------------------------
22. GLOBAL VARIABLES — BE CAREFUL
------------------------------------------------------------

Although globals can be useful, excessive use creates problems:

    - hidden dependencies
    - harder testing
    - harder debugging
    - unexpected modifications
    - poor modularity

Prefer passing required data through function parameters when
practical.


------------------------------------------------------------
23. SCOPE VS LIFETIME
------------------------------------------------------------

These are different concepts.

SCOPE:
    Where a name can be accessed in source code.

LIFETIME:
    How long the object exists during program execution.

Example:

void test(void)
{
    int x = 10;
}

`x` has block scope.

Its lifetime normally lasts from entering the block until
execution leaves the block.


------------------------------------------------------------
24. STATIC LOCAL VARIABLE
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

#include <stdio.h>

void counter(void)
{
    static int count = 0;

    count++;

    printf("%d\n", count);
}

int main(void)
{
    counter();
    counter();
    counter();

    return 0;
}

Output:

1
2
3

Why?

A static local variable:

    - has block scope
    - retains its value between function calls
    - has static storage duration
    - is initialized only once


------------------------------------------------------------
25. NORMAL LOCAL VS STATIC LOCAL
------------------------------------------------------------

Normal:

void test(void)
{
    int x = 0;
    x++;
    printf("%d\n", x);
}

Calling repeatedly:

1
1
1


Static:

void test(void)
{
    static int x = 0;
    x++;
    printf("%d\n", x);
}

Calling repeatedly:

