26. RECURSION
------------------------------------------------------------

Recursion means a function calls itself.

Example:

void countdown(int n)
{
    if (n == 0)
        return;

    printf("%d\n", n);

    countdown(n - 1);
}


Call:

countdown(3);

Execution:

countdown(3)
    |
    +--> print 3
    |
    +--> countdown(2)
             |
             +--> print 2
             |
             +--> countdown(1)
                      |
                      +--> print 1
                      |
                      +--> countdown(0)
                               |
                               +--> return


Output:

3
2
1


------------------------------------------------------------
27. BASE CASE IN RECURSION
------------------------------------------------------------

Every useful recursive algorithm needs a termination
condition.

Example:

int factorial(int n)
{
    if (n <= 1)
        return 1;

    return n * factorial(n - 1);
}

factorial(5):

5 * factorial(4)
    4 * factorial(3)
        3 * factorial(2)
            2 * factorial(1)
                1

Result:

120


------------------------------------------------------------
28. RECURSION CALL STACK
------------------------------------------------------------

Each function invocation generally gets its own activation
record/stack frame when automatic storage is involved.

factorial(4)

Stack:

+------------------+
| factorial(1)     |
+------------------+
| factorial(2)     |
+------------------+
| factorial(3)     |
+------------------+
| factorial(4)     |
+------------------+
| main()           |
+------------------+

After the base case, frames return in reverse order.


------------------------------------------------------------
29. RECURSION DEPTH
------------------------------------------------------------

Deep recursion can consume significant stack space.

Example:

void infinite(void)
{
    infinite();
}

There is no base case.

Eventually the program may fail because of stack exhaustion.

Therefore recursion must have:

    - correct base case
    - progress toward base case
    - reasonable depth


------------------------------------------------------------
30. ITERATION VS RECURSION
------------------------------------------------------------

Factorial iteratively:

int factorial(int n)
{
    int result = 1;

    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++)
        result *= i;

    return result;
}


Recursive:

int factorial(int n)
{
    if (n <= 1)
        return 1;

    return n * factorial(n - 1);
}

Iteration often uses less stack space.

Recursion can make some algorithms easier to express.

Common recursive algorithms:

    - tree traversal
    - DFS
    - divide and conquer
    - merge sort
    - quicksort
    - backtracking


