42. FUNCTION DECLARATION VS DEFINITION
------------------------------------------------------------

Declaration:

int add(int, int);

Definition:

int add(int a, int b)
{
    return a + b;
}

Declaration says:

    "This function exists."

Definition says:

    "This is what the function does."


------------------------------------------------------------
43. FUNCTION OVERLOADING IN C
------------------------------------------------------------

C does NOT support function overloading like C++.

You cannot write:

int add(int a, int b);

float add(float a, float b);

with the same function name and expect C to select based on
argument types.

Instead use different names:

int add_int(int a, int b);

float add_float(float a, float b);


------------------------------------------------------------
44. DEFAULT ARGUMENTS IN C
------------------------------------------------------------

C does NOT support C++-style default arguments.

Not valid C:

int add(int a, int b = 10);


Instead explicitly pass required arguments or design a
different API.


------------------------------------------------------------
45. NESTED FUNCTIONS
------------------------------------------------------------

Standard C does not support defining a function inside another
function.

Not standard C:

void outer(void)
{
    void inner(void)
    {
    }
}

Some compilers provide non-standard extensions, but avoid them
for portable C programming.


------------------------------------------------------------
46. FUNCTION REENTRANCY — BASIC IDEA
------------------------------------------------------------

A reentrant function can safely be called again before a
previous invocation has completed, under appropriate conditions.

Avoid relying on:

    - shared mutable global state
    - static local state

when writing functions intended for concurrent or interrupt-
driven environments.

This becomes important in:

    - operating systems
    - embedded systems
    - multithreading
    - signal handling


------------------------------------------------------------
47. PURE-STYLE FUNCTIONS
------------------------------------------------------------

A function is easier to reason about when:

    output depends only on inputs
    and
    it does not modify external state.

Example:

int square(int x)
{
    return x * x;
}

This has no global-state dependency.

Such functions are easier to:

    - test
    - debug
    - reuse
    - reason about


------------------------------------------------------------
48. COMMON FUNCTION BUGS
------------------------------------------------------------

BUG 1:
Forgetting return value.

int add(int a, int b)
{
    a + b;     // wrong if result is expected
}


Correct:

return a + b;


BUG 2:
Wrong return type.

int getValue(void)
{
    return 3.14;   // conversion occurs
}

If precision matters, use an appropriate type.


BUG 3:
Missing prototype.

Calling a function without a correct visible declaration
can cause serious problems, especially when argument/return
types do not match expectations.


BUG 4:
Returning address of a local variable.

int *getValue(void)
{
    int x = 10;
    return &x;     // WRONG
}

After the function returns, x's lifetime has ended.


BUG 5:
Incorrect pointer usage.

void change(int *p)
{
    p = NULL;      // changes only local pointer copy
}

This does not change the caller's pointer.


BUG 6:
Infinite recursion.

void test(void)
{
    test();
}


BUG 7:
Incorrect array size calculation.

void test(int arr[])
{
    sizeof(arr);   // size of pointer, not original array
}


------------------------------------------------------------
49. RETURNING POINTERS FROM FUNCTIONS
------------------------------------------------------------

This is valid if the returned pointer points to an object that
continues to exist after the function returns.

BAD:

int *getNumber(void)
{
    int x = 10;
    return &x;
}


Potentially valid:

int *getStaticNumber(void)
{
    static int x = 10;
    return &x;
}


But using static storage introduces shared state, so design
carefully.


Another valid approach is dynamically allocated memory:

int *createNumber(void)
{
    int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);

    if (p != NULL)
        *p = 10;

    return p;
}

The caller must eventually free the allocated memory.


------------------------------------------------------------
50. CONST PARAMETERS
------------------------------------------------------------

If a function should not modify data through a pointer,
use const.

Example:

void printArray(const int arr[], int n)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
}

This communicates the function's intention and lets the
compiler diagnose accidental modification through arr.


------------------------------------------------------------
51. CONST WITH POINTER PARAMETERS
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

const int *p;

Means:

    pointer to const int

You cannot modify the int through p.

Example:

void display(const int *p)
{
    printf("%d", *p);
}


------------------------------------------------------------
52. FUNCTION PARAMETER QUALIFIERS
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

void display(const int *arr, size_t n);

This says:

    arr points to int objects that this function promises not
    to modify through arr.

For job interviews, understand the difference between:

    int *p
    const int *p
    int *const p
    const int *const p


------------------------------------------------------------
53. FUNCTION POINTER VS NORMAL POINTER
------------------------------------------------------------

Normal pointer:

int *p;

p points to an int object.


Function pointer:

int (*fp)(int, int);

fp points to a function accepting two int parameters and
returning int.


------------------------------------------------------------
54. FUNCTION POINTER TABLE
------------------------------------------------------------

Function pointers can implement dispatch tables.

Example:

#include <stdio.h>

int add(int a, int b)
{
    return a + b;
}

int sub(int a, int b)
{
    return a - b;
}

int mul(int a, int b)
{
    return a * b;
}

int main(void)
{
    int (*operations[])(int, int) =
    {
        add,
        sub,
        mul
    };

    printf("%d\n", operations[0](10, 5));
    printf("%d\n", operations[1](10, 5));
    printf("%d\n", operations[2](10, 5));

    return 0;
}

Output:

15
5
50


Useful for:

    - menu systems
    - command interpreters
    - state machines
    - embedded systems
    - parsers


