------------------------------------------------------------
56. STACK FRAME
------------------------------------------------------------

When a function is called, the implementation generally needs
to maintain information associated with that invocation.

Conceptually:

main()
  |
  +--> add()
         |
         +--> parameters
         +--> local variables
         +--> return information


Important:

The exact stack-frame layout is implementation/ABI dependent.

Do not assume every compiler uses exactly the same layout.


------------------------------------------------------------
57. FUNCTION CALL FLOW
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

int add(int a, int b)
{
    return a + b;
}

int main(void)
{
    int x = add(10, 20);
}

Flow:

main()
 |
 | call add(10,20)
 v
add()
 |
 | calculate 10 + 20
 |
 | return 30
 v
main()
 |
 x = 30


------------------------------------------------------------
58. RECURSIVE FACTORIAL — COMPLETE PROGRAM
------------------------------------------------------------

#include <stdio.h>

long long factorial(int n)
{
    if (n < 0)
        return -1;

    if (n <= 1)
        return 1;

    return (long long)n * factorial(n - 1);
}

int main(void)
{
    int n = 5;

    printf("Factorial = %lld\n", factorial(n));

    return 0;
}

Output:

Factorial = 120

NOTE:

Even long long has a finite range. Large factorials overflow
the type.


------------------------------------------------------------
59. PRIME CHECK USING FUNCTION
------------------------------------------------------------

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

bool isPrime(int n)
{
    if (n < 2)
        return false;

    for (int i = 2; i <= n / i; i++)
    {
        if (n % i == 0)
            return false;
    }

    return true;
}

int main(void)
{
    int n = 29;

    if (isPrime(n))
        printf("Prime\n");
    else
        printf("Not Prime\n");

    return 0;
}


Why:

i <= n / i

instead of:

i * i <= n

The division form avoids multiplication overflow for integer
types in cases where i*i could overflow.


------------------------------------------------------------
60. GCD USING FUNCTION
------------------------------------------------------------

#include <stdio.h>

int gcd(int a, int b)
{
    while (b != 0)
    {
        int temp = a % b;
        a = b;
        b = temp;
    }

    return a;
}

int main(void)
{
    printf("%d\n", gcd(48, 18));

    return 0;
}

Output:

6

This is Euclid's algorithm.


------------------------------------------------------------
61. LINEAR SEARCH USING FUNCTION
------------------------------------------------------------

int linearSearch(const int arr[], int n, int key)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        if (arr[i] == key)
            return i;
    }

    return -1;
}

Example:

int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40};

int index = linearSearch(arr, 4, 30);

index = 2


------------------------------------------------------------
62. MAXIMUM ELEMENT USING FUNCTION
------------------------------------------------------------

int findMax(const int arr[], int n)
{
    if (n <= 0)
        return 0;   // Better API design may use a status result.

    int max = arr[0];

    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++)
    {
        if (arr[i] > max)
            max = arr[i];
    }

    return max;
}


IMPORTANT JOB CONCEPT:

Always think about invalid input.

What should happen when:

    n == 0
    arr == NULL

A production-quality API should define its contract clearly.


------------------------------------------------------------
63. MULTIPLE RETURN VALUES
------------------------------------------------------------

C functions can directly return only one value.

But multiple results can be produced through pointers.

Example:

void calculate(int a, int b, int *sum, int *product)
{
    *sum = a + b;
    *product = a * b;
}

int main(void)
{
    int sum;
    int product;

    calculate(5, 4, &sum, &product);

    printf("Sum = %d\n", sum);
    printf("Product = %d\n", product);
}


------------------------------------------------------------
64. BETTER API DESIGN
------------------------------------------------------------

A good function should generally have:

    - clear name
    - clear parameters
    - clear return value
    - documented assumptions
    - predictable behavior
    - limited side effects
    - proper error handling


Poor:

void process(int x, int y, int z, int a, int b, int c);


Better:

Use meaningful structures when many related values are passed.


------------------------------------------------------------
65. STRUCTURES WITH FUNCTIONS
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

struct Student
{
    char name[50];
    int age;
    float marks;
};

void displayStudent(const struct Student *s)
{
    printf("%s %d %.2f\n",
           s->name,
           s->age,
           s->marks);
}

Using a pointer avoids copying the whole structure and
`const` prevents modification.


------------------------------------------------------------
66. FUNCTION DESIGN — SINGLE RESPONSIBILITY
------------------------------------------------------------

Prefer:

readInput()
validateInput()
calculateResult()
displayResult()

rather than:

doEverything()

Example:

int calculateTotal(int price, int quantity)
{
    return price * quantity;
}

One function = one clear responsibility.


------------------------------------------------------------
67. ERROR HANDLING THROUGH RETURN VALUES
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

int divide(int a, int b, int *result)
{
    if (b == 0)
        return 0;

    *result = a / b;

    return 1;
}

Usage:

int result;

if (divide(10, 2, &result))
{
    printf("%d\n", result);
}
else
{
    printf("Division failed\n");
}

A more expressive production API might use an enum/status
code rather than a simple 0/1.


------------------------------------------------------------
68. FUNCTION COMPLEXITY
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

int sum(const int arr[], int n)
{
    int sum = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        sum += arr[i];

    return sum;
}

Time complexity:

O(n)

Space complexity:

O(1)

Breaking code into functions does NOT automatically change
algorithmic complexity.


------------------------------------------------------------
69. FUNCTION REUSE
------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose:

calculateTax()
calculateSalary()
calculateBonus()

A reusable function:

double calculateTax(double salary)
{
    ...
}

can be used from multiple parts of the application.


