------------------------------------------------------------
84. GATE-LEVEL CONCEPT
------------------------------------------------------------

Consider:

void f(int *p)
{
    *p = 20;
}

int main(void)
{
    int x = 10;

    f(&x);

    printf("%d", x);
}

Question:

Why does x become 20?

Answer:

    &x
      |
      v
    address of x
      |
      v
    p

Then:

    *p

accesses the object x.

Therefore:

    *p = 20

modifies x itself.


